## 9. Tuning and scaling

### 9.1 Vertical scaling

The web app and the worker are separate processes for a reason: a browser
execution pegging a CPU should not slow the API. Run them on the same host
for a small install and split them once the worker's CPU or memory
pressure affects the API's latency.

The most memory-hungry path is browser execution. A single Chromium
context at the default 1440×900 viewport at 2× DPR is roughly 150–300 MB.
`PLAYWRIGHT_MAX_CONTEXTS` (default 2) caps concurrency accordingly.

### 9.2 Horizontal scaling of the worker

Multiple workers on multiple hosts all poll the same Postgres `jobs`
table. There is no leader election: the row-level lock in the dequeue is
the only coordination. Add workers until the queue's steady-state depth
stops falling; past that point the workers contend on the lock and
throughput plateaus.

Point every worker at the same `DATABASE_URL` and the same
`STORAGE_DRIVER` configuration. They do not need to share a filesystem
when `STORAGE_DRIVER=s3`; they must share `STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT` (a network
filesystem or a replica) when `STORAGE_DRIVER=local`, because any worker
may be asked to serve any artifact.

### 9.3 Redis (optional)

Redis is not required. When `REDIS_URL` is set, the queue module uses it
for a wake-up notification so a worker does not wait for the poll
interval before starting a job. Without Redis, the poll interval
(`JOBS_POLL_INTERVAL_MS`, default 1 s) is the latency floor between an
enqueue and a worker starting it. Neither changes correctness: the
Postgres row is the source of truth and the lock is the only coordination.

